# builds.slice: where resource-heavy builds run, with a ceiling they can actually hit.
#
# Builds used to run wherever they were launched, which on work-box meant inside
# user-0.slice alongside every interactive session. On 2026-08-20 two Widget builds
# and eight interactive sessions pinned that slice against MemoryHigh=8G for 1h42m: 92%
# system CPU, ~2 GB/s of re-reads, file cache reclaimed to 1.8 MB, and oom_kill 0.
# Nothing died, so nothing recovered.
#
# Moving builds here fixes the SHARING problem: a build no longer competes with the
# sessions for one slice's budget, so it cannot starve the terminals you would need in
# order to go and kill it.
#
# DELIBERATELY NO MemoryHigh. That is the lesson of the incident, not an oversight.
# A brake below a wall holds the cgroup below the wall by reclaiming, which means the
# wall is never reached and the in-cgroup OOM killer never fires. For interactive work
# a brake is right: going slow beats being killed. For a BUILD the opposite is true:
# a build that does not fit should die immediately and say so, because a build that
# merely goes slow is indistinguishable from a hung one and costs hours before anyone
# looks. Fail fast, fail alone, fail legibly.
#
# MemoryMax=6G against a 15G box: leaves ~10G for user-0.slice's real steady state
# (~3G of sessions) plus docker/system (~3G) with room to spare. MemorySwapMax bounds
# the thrash on the way to the ceiling: an unbounded swap turns a clean kill into the
# 16h stall recorded on 2026-08-18.
[Unit]
Description=Resource-heavy builds (serialised by orrery heavy-build)
Before=slices.target

[Slice]
MemoryAccounting=yes
MemoryMax=6G
MemorySwapMax=512M
# Builds are batch work: yield CPU and IO to interactive sessions rather than
# competing evenly with them.
CPUWeight=20
IOWeight=20
